  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 AM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nThe broad upper troughing over the Great Lakes continues to \nshift east into the northeast CONUS this morning which is \nallowing a surface high to build into the region from the Upper \nMidwest. This surface high is expected to remain over the area \nthrough Tuesday which will result in rain-free conditions and \ncomfortable humidity. However, the persistent north-northwest \nwinds will keep temperatures on the seasonably cool side today \nand Monday with afternoon highs in the mid to upper 70s and \novernight lows in the mid to upper 50s to locally near 60. Winds\nare forecast to become more southerly on Tuesday which should \nallow temperatures to rebound back into the 80s. \n\nSpeaking of winds, north-northwesterly gusts around 20 mph are \nexpected this afternoon with locally higher gusts possible over \nthe open waters of Lake Michigan. Despite ongoing breezy winds \n(10-15 kt) over the lake, waves have been rather slow to respond\nwith current buoy observations showing 2.0-2.5 ft waves \noffshore of Cook County and 3.0-3.5 ft waves offshore of \nnorthwest IN. That said, as the north-northwest winds prevail it\nstill looks as if wave heights should continue to increase \nthrough this afternoon resulting in an increasing risk for rip \ncurrents and dangerous swimming conditions, especially at \nnorthwest IN beaches. Therefore, the current plan is to maintain\nthe ongoing Beach Hazard Statements for Porter, Lake (IN), and \nCook Counties through this evening for this threat. So if you \nare planning to head to the beach today be sure to check the \nlatest surf forecast beforehand and remember to stay dry where \nthe waves are high! \n\nOur next chance for rain is not expected until the middle of \nthe week when the shortwave trough currently over the Pacific \nNorthwest is forecast to pivot through the Great Lakes. \nAssociated with the shortwave is also expected to be a cold \nfront which will be the main driver of the anticipated shower \nand thunderstorm activity. The latest trend in the 00z guidance \nsuite is for the front to begin to move into northern IL late \nTuesday night with a decaying area of showers and thunderstorms \nalong it. Given the late night arrival and the lingering mid- \nlevel dry air forecast to be overhead locally, it seems that the\nbest coverage of any showers/storms Tuesday night will be near \nthe IL-WI line. The front is then forecast to drift across\nnorthern IL and northwest IN on Wednesday which should allow \nfor the redevelopment of showers and thunderstorms Wednesday \nafternoon and evening as diurnal destabilization occurs. While \nforecast soundings do show some decent MLCAPE values (around \n1000-2000 J/kg) and steep mid-level lapse rates, the convergence\nalong the frontal boundary looks to be rather meager which may \nlimit the coverage of showers and storms on Wednesday. That \nsaid, forecast soundings also show around 25-35 kts of deep \nlayer shear Wednesday afternoon which may allow a few storms to \nbecome strong to possibly severe. At this time our area is not \noutlooked for severe weather likely due to uncertainty in storm \ncoverage and timing, but this will be a period to monitor as we \nget closer. \n\nThe cold front is forecast to exit the area late Wednesday \nnight into Thursday morning which looks to allow another surface\nhigh to build into the area. Though, with some uncertainty as \nto how fast the front will exit this could change in subsequent \nforecasts. Regardless, another period of dry weather should \ndevelop to close out the work week, but there is a signal for \nmore active weather to return towards the end of the weekend \ninto the early part of the following week. On the bright side, \ntemperatures do look to stay seasonable through the week with \ndaily highs in the 80s and overnight lows in the 60s.\n\nYack",
